## 1. Why Accuracy Alone is a Lie

### The Problem
If your dataset is imbalanced, accuracy gives you a false sense of performance.

```python
# Example — Disease Detection
# 95 out of 100 people DON'T have disease

# A lazy model that ALWAYS predicts "No Disease"
def lazy_model(patient):
    return "No Disease"

# Accuracy = 95/100 = 95% — but model is completely useless!
# It never detects a single disease case
```

### Real World Imbalanced Datasets
| Problem | Majority Class | Minority Class |
|---|---|---|
| Fraud Detection | Genuine (99%) | Fraud (1%) |
| Cancer Detection | Normal (95%) | Cancer (5%) |
| Spam Detection | Not Spam (80%) | Spam (20%) |

> **Rule:** Always check class distribution before trusting accuracy.
> If data is imbalanced → use Precision, Recall, F1 instead.

### Analogy — Cricket Fielder 🏏
A fielder who never attempts a catch has a 0% drop rate.
Looks perfect on paper — completely useless in reality.
Accuracy is that drop rate.

